    "\n",
    "## 1. Introduction: Why We Need Better Tools <a id='introduction'></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "### The Big Picture\n",
    "\n",
    "Mathematics is full of **objects**:\n",
    "- A polynomial like $p(x) = x^2 + 2x + 1$\n",
    "- A vector like $\\vec{v} = [3, 4]$\n",
    "- A matrix like $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$\n",
    "\n",
    "These aren't just numbers—they have:\n",
    "- **Properties** (degree, magnitude, determinant)\n",
    "- **Operations** (addition, multiplication, derivatives)\n",
    "- **Behaviors** (evaluation, rotation, transformation)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Our Journey\n",
    "\n",
    "We'll start simple and build up:\n",
    "1. **Functions and lists** (the \"obvious\" way)\n",
    "2. **Where it gets messy** (tracking state, repetitive code)\n",
    "3. **Object-oriented approach** (cleaner, more natural)\n",
    "4. **Why libraries do it this way** (NumPy, SymPy)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why This Matters for Machine Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "Machine learning is built on these mathematical objects:\n",
    "- **Vectors**: data points, features, parameters, gradients\n",
    "- **Matrices**: transformations, weight matrices\n",
    "- **Functions**: loss functions, activation functions\n",
    "\n",
    "Understanding how to structure mathematical code will make you better at:\n",
    "- Reading ML library source code\n",
    "- Implementing custom operations\n",
    "- Debugging mathematical issues"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from typing import List, Tuple\n",
    "from math import sqrt\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plotting setup\n",
    "plt.style.use('seaborn-v0_8-darkgrid')\n",
    "plt.rcParams['figure.figsize'] = (10, 6)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2. Polynomials: Starting with Simple Functions <a id='polynomials-functions'></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's start with polynomials. A polynomial is just:\n",
    "$$p(x) = a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2 + \\cdots + a_n x^n$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Approach 1: Using a List of Coefficients\n",
    "\n",
    "The simplest way: store coefficients in a list."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def evaluate_polynomial(coefficients: List[float], x: float) -> float:\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Evaluate a polynomial at a given point.\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        coefficients: [a0, a1, a2, ...] for a0 + a1*x + a2*x^2 + ...\n",
    "        x: Point at which to evaluate\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "        The value p(x)\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    result = 0\n",
    "    for power, coeff in enumerate(coefficients):\n",
    "        result += coeff * (x ** power)\n",
    "    return result\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example: p(x) = 1 + 2x + x^2\n",
    "poly_coeffs = [1, 2, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Polynomial: p(x) = 1 + 2x + x^2\")\n",
    "print(f\"Coefficients: {poly_coeffs}\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\np(0) = {evaluate_polynomial(poly_coeffs, 0)}\")\n",
    "print(f\"p(1) = {evaluate_polynomial(poly_coeffs, 1)}\")\n",
    "print(f\"p(2) = {evaluate_polynomial(poly_coeffs, 2)}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Visualize\n",
    "x_values = np.linspace(-3, 1, 100)\n",
    "y_values = [evaluate_polynomial(poly_coeffs, x) for x in x_values]\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.plot(x_values, y_values, 'b-', linewidth=2, label='p(x) = 1 + 2x + x²')\n",
    "plt.scatter([0, 1, 2], [evaluate_polynomial(poly_coeffs, x) for x in [0, 1, 2]], \n",
    "           color='red', s=100, zorder=5, label='Evaluated points')\n",
    "plt.axhline(y=0, color='k', linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.axvline(x=0, color='k', linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('p(x)')\n",
    "plt.title('Polynomial Evaluation')\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n✓ This works! But let's try doing more...\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Adding Polynomials\n",
    "\n",
    "What if we want to add two polynomials? $(1 + 2x + x^2) + (3 + x) = 4 + 3x + x^2$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def add_polynomials(coeffs1: List[float], coeffs2: List[float]) -> List[float]:\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Add two polynomials.\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        coeffs1: Coefficients of first polynomial\n",
    "        coeffs2: Coefficients of second polynomial\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "        Coefficients of sum\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    # Make them the same length by padding with zeros\n",
    "    max_len = max(len(coeffs1), len(coeffs2))\n",
    "    padded1 = coeffs1 + [0] * (max_len - len(coeffs1))\n",
    "    padded2 = coeffs2 + [0] * (max_len - len(coeffs2))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Add corresponding coefficients\n",
    "    result = [c1 + c2 for c1, c2 in zip(padded1, padded2)]\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return result\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example\n",
    "poly1 = [1, 2, 1]  # 1 + 2x + x^2\n",
    "poly2 = [3, 1]     # 3 + x\n",
    "\n",
    "poly_sum = add_polynomials(poly1, poly2)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Addition:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  (1 + 2x + x²) + (3 + x)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  = {poly_sum}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  = {poly_sum[0]} + {poly_sum[1]}x + {poly_sum[2]}x²\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Verify by evaluation\n",
    "test_x = 2\n",
    "result1 = evaluate_polynomial(poly1, test_x)\n",
    "result2 = evaluate_polynomial(poly2, test_x)\n",
    "result_sum = evaluate_polynomial(poly_sum, test_x)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"\\nVerification at x = {test_x}:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  p₁({test_x}) = {result1}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  p₂({test_x}) = {result2}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Sum = {result_sum}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Check: {result1} + {result2} = {result1 + result2} ✓\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Multiplying Polynomials\n",
    "\n",
    "This gets more complex: $(1 + x)(1 + 2x) = 1 + 3x + 2x^2$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def multiply_polynomials(coeffs1: List[float], coeffs2: List[float]) -> List[float]:\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Multiply two polynomials using convolution.\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        coeffs1: Coefficients of first polynomial\n",
    "        coeffs2: Coefficients of second polynomial\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "        Coefficients of product\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    # Result will have degree = sum of degrees\n",
    "    result_degree = (len(coeffs1) - 1) + (len(coeffs2) - 1)\n",
    "    result = [0] * (result_degree + 1)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Multiply each term in coeffs1 by each term in coeffs2\n",
    "    for i, c1 in enumerate(coeffs1):\n",
    "        for j, c2 in enumerate(coeffs2):\n",
    "            result[i + j] += c1 * c2\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return result\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example\n",
    "poly_a = [1, 1]     # 1 + x\n",
    "poly_b = [1, 2]     # 1 + 2x\n",
    "\n",
    "poly_product = multiply_polynomials(poly_a, poly_b)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Multiplication:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  (1 + x)(1 + 2x)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  = {poly_product}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  = 1 + 3x + 2x²\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Verify\n",
    "test_x = 3\n",
    "val_a = evaluate_polynomial(poly_a, test_x)\n",
    "val_b = evaluate_polynomial(poly_b, test_x)\n",
    "val_product = evaluate_polynomial(poly_product, test_x)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"\\nVerification at x = {test_x}:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  (1 + x) = {val_a}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  (1 + 2x) = {val_b}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Product = {val_product}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Check: {val_a} × {val_b} = {val_a * val_b} ✓\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Taking Derivatives\n",
    "\n",
    "The derivative of $a_n x^n$ is $n \\cdot a_n x^{n-1}$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def derivative_polynomial(coefficients: List[float]) -> List[float]:\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Compute the derivative of a polynomial.\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        coefficients: Polynomial coefficients\n",
    "    \n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "        Coefficients of derivative\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    if len(coefficients) <= 1:\n",
    "        return [0]  # Derivative of constant is 0\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Apply power rule: d/dx(a*x^n) = n*a*x^(n-1)\n",
    "    result = [i * coefficients[i] for i in range(1, len(coefficients))]\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return result\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example: p(x) = x^3 - 2x^2 + x + 5\n",
    "poly = [5, 1, -2, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Original: p(x) = 5 + x - 2x² + x³\")\n",
    "print(f\"Coefficients: {poly}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Take derivatives\n",
    "first_deriv = derivative_polynomial(poly)\n",
    "print(f\"\\nFirst derivative: p'(x) = {first_deriv}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  = 1 - 4x + 3x²\")\n",
    "\n",
    "second_deriv = derivative_polynomial(first_deriv)\n",
    "print(f\"\\nSecond derivative: p''(x) = {second_deriv}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  = -4 + 6x\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Visualize\n",
    "x_vals = np.linspace(-2, 3, 100)\n",
    "y_orig = [evaluate_polynomial(poly, x) for x in x_vals]\n",
    "y_deriv = [evaluate_polynomial(first_deriv, x) for x in x_vals]\n",
    "y_deriv2 = [evaluate_polynomial(second_deriv, x) for x in x_vals]\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 1)\n",
    "plt.plot(x_vals, y_orig, 'b-', linewidth=2)\n",
    "plt.axhline(0, color='k', linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.axvline(0, color='k', linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.title('p(x)')\n",
    "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('y')\n",
    "plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 2)\n",
    "plt.plot(x_vals, y_deriv, 'r-', linewidth=2)\n",
    "plt.axhline(0, color='k', linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.axvline(0, color='k', linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.title(\"p'(x)\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('y')\n",
    "plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.subplot(1, 3, 3)\n",
    "plt.plot(x_vals, y_deriv2, 'g-', linewidth=2)\n",
    "plt.axhline(0, color='k', linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.axvline(0, color='k', linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.title(\"p''(x)\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel('x')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('y')\n",
    "plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
